WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:05.297
Good evening, America.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.133
There are decisions that we are

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.760
asked to make in our lifetimes

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.094
that are truly humbling.

00:00:12.137 --> 00:00:13.931
Inside that building right now.

00:00:13.931 --> 00:00:17.142
The powers that be are
negotiating your future.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:19.603
Burdick: 
Decisions that in previous times

00:00:19.603 --> 00:00:21.480
would have been
left to the gods.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.981
Hologram: 
But you can’t negotiate

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:24.066
with a fire,

00:00:24.066 --> 00:00:25.067
or a flood,

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:25.901
or a famine.

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:29.238
We must save ourselves.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:34.952
Extrapolations has sort
of an interesting

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:36.036
origin story.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.956
After I was involved in the

00:00:38.956 --> 00:00:40.582
making of An Inconvenient Truth,

00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:41.959
one of the questions

00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:44.586
that I asked myself was,

00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:48.257
as a storyteller, how do
we take the science

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:50.968
and move it into more

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:53.053
conventional, scripted
storytelling?

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.097
In episode one, we
really established

00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:56.265
the chaos of the world.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.184
Sienna Miller: 
There's famine, there are fires.

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:03.230
The world is a pretty
unsafe place to live in.

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:07.901
Scott Burns: Anytime you’re 
looking into the future,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.070
you're asked to make
some speculations.

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:13.407
How's the world gonna look?

00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:14.491
What's gonna change?

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:16.827
It’s an enormous theme

00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:18.578
played on an
enormous stage

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.581
with very personal,
small stories,

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.333
which is why I think
it's so relatable.

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:26.169
The shows that pretend that the

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:27.963
climate is going to be the same

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.965
in ten or fifteen years,

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:32.592
those are the science
fiction shows because

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.802
the climate is changing.

00:01:34.303 --> 00:01:36.888
The nature of the show

00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:38.432
lends itself to

00:01:38.432 --> 00:01:40.434
really not being able to know
what you're gonna get.

00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:41.727
The cliffhangers aren't for

00:01:41.727 --> 00:01:43.103
the immediate moment after

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.314
it's for what's gonna
happen in five years.

00:01:46.315 --> 00:01:48.191
My family's never
leaving, you know.

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:49.276
We would come and hear you speak

00:01:49.276 --> 00:01:51.445
even if we had to tread
water the entire time.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:54.239
That's a comforting
thought.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:55.574
It's very vivid.

00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:57.826
One of the great things
about the stories in here

00:01:57.826 --> 00:01:59.536
is that it manages to
shrink them down

00:01:59.536 --> 00:02:00.829
to small personal moments.

00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:02.622
Scott Burns:
The cast is huge.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.917
The first person I
spoke to was Meryl.

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.086
She read the script

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:09.671
and said, "okay, when
are we doing this?"

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.214
Sienna Miller

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:13.842
was so excited to play
Meryl Streep’s daughter.

00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:15.552
You want me to read
for a while, mom?

00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:17.137
No, no, no. I'm-

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.850
You're going to be here.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:22.559
I'm not.

00:02:22.768 --> 00:02:24.853
Scott Burns:
Yara Shahidi is very powerful.

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:26.772
Hari Neff is spectacular.

00:02:27.022 --> 00:02:27.689
Edward Norton

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:30.567
is about as passionate
and as eloquent

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.110
a person as I've ever met.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:32.903
Gemma Chan,

00:02:32.903 --> 00:02:34.029
Tahar Rahim,

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:34.905
Diane Lane,

00:02:34.905 --> 00:02:35.906
Tobey Maguire,

00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:36.990
Eiza Gonzales,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.075
Forest Whitaker,

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:39.159
Marion Cotillard,

00:02:39.451 --> 00:02:40.160
and Kit.

00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:42.162
Kit Harington is sort of

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.082
the connective tissue
in this entire series.

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:46.917
Good evening, everyone.

00:02:47.084 --> 00:02:47.709
I hope

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:50.504
people can find every
emotion in it,

00:02:50.504 --> 00:02:51.380
including hope.

00:02:51.922 --> 00:02:54.132
I think the way that
you find comfort

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:57.177
is imagining yourself
in the positions that

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.345
our characters are in.

00:02:58.345 --> 00:02:59.429
We’re not gods.

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:00.722
We're just parents and children,

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:02.015
please come home, pal.

00:03:02.224 --> 00:03:03.350
Daveed Diggs:
I hope people are entertained,

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.476
I certainly am.

00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:06.395
The scripts are
beautifully shot.

00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:08.814
There's jokes like, it's funny.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.650
Dorothy Fortenberry:
The show, ultimately,

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.985
every episode is about

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.737
people finding each other,

00:03:15.737 --> 00:03:18.198
people finding ways to team up.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.075
New friends, fellow citizens,

00:03:20.075 --> 00:03:21.785
and they can find a
way to work together.

